      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "In a standard patient, skin cancer often presents as a new or changing mole, which can be a suspicious-looking lesion with irregular borders, uneven color, or an unusual diameter. In contrast, in a black patient, melanoma, the most aggressive form of skin cancer, often presents as a flat, black lesion, which can be difficult to distinguish from a benign growth or a freckle. This is because black skin has less melanin, making it harder to detect the cancerous cells, and the lesions may appear more like a mole or a birthmark.",
